        {
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Mon, ma, mes — saying \"my\"",
              "body": "French has three words for \"my\". The choice depends on the noun that follows, not on you: \"mon\" before masculine nouns (mon père), \"ma\" before feminine nouns (ma mère), \"mes\" before any plural (mes parents). Before a feminine word starting with a vowel, use \"mon\" for easier pronunciation: mon amie.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "mon père, mon frère", "english": "my father, my brother", "note": "masculine" },
                { "french": "ma mère, ma sœur", "english": "my mother, my sister", "note": "feminine" },
                { "french": "mes parents, mes sœurs", "english": "my parents, my sisters", "note": "plural" },
                { "french": "mon amie", "english": "my friend (female)", "note": "feminine + vowel → mon" },
                { "french": "mon oncle, ma tante", "english": "my uncle, my aunt" },
                { "french": "mes grands-parents", "english": "my grandparents", "note": "plural" }
              ],
              "tip": "Never say \"ma amie\" — two vowels clash, so French switches to \"mon amie\" even though \"amie\" is feminine."
            }
          ]
        },
